Here is an example of Rust code that generates a prime number stream using the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm:
```
use std::collections::VecDeque;

struct PrimeGenerator {
    sieve: Vec<bool>,
}

impl PrimeGenerator {
    fn new(n: usize) -> PrimeGenerator {
        let sieve = vec![true; n + 1];
        sieve[0] = sieve[1] = false;
        PrimeGenerator { sieve }
    }

    fn generate(&mut self) -> Option<usize> {
        for i in 2..=self.sieve.len() {
            if self.sieve[i] {
                let mut j = i * i;
                while j <= self.sieve.len() {
                    self.sieve[j] = false;
                    j += i;
                }
                return Some(i);
            }
        }
        None
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut generator = PrimeGenerator::new(100); // generate primes up to 100
    let mut primes = VecDeque::new();

    loop {
        match generator.generate() {
            Some(prime) => primes.push_back(prime),
            None => break,
        }
    }

    for prime in primes {
        println!("{}", prime);
    }
}
```
This code defines a `PrimeGenerator` struct that uses a boolean vector `sieve` to keep track of prime numbers. The `generate` method iterates over the sieve, marking as composite (i.e., not prime) any multiple of a prime number starting from 2. The first prime number in the range is then returned.

In the `main` function, we create a `PrimeGenerator` instance and a `VecDeque` to store the generated primes. We then enter a loop that calls `generate` until it returns `None`, indicating that there are no more primes in the range. We print out the generated primes.

You can adjust the `n` parameter in the `new` method to change the range of prime numbers generated.

Note that this implementation has a time complexity of O(n log log n) and a space complexity of O(n), making it suitable for generating primes up to a reasonable limit. For larger ranges, you may want to consider more advanced algorithms or libraries.

