Here is a C# program that calculates pi up to 5 decimals using the Bailey-Borwein-Plouffe formula (BBP formula) and then XOR's the result twice.

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Calculate pi using BBP formula
        double pi = CalculatePi(1000); // Increase the number to improve accuracy

        // Round pi to 5 decimals
        pi = Math.Round(pi, 5);

        // Convert pi to a binary string
        string binaryPi = Convert.ToDouble(pi).ToString("B");

        // XOR the binary string twice
        string result = XorString(binaryPi);

        Console.WriteLine($"Pi up to 5 decimals: {pi}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Binary Pi: {binaryPi}");
        Console.WriteLine($"XOR'd Pi: {result}");
    }

    static double CalculatePi(int iterations)
    {
        double pi = 0;
        for (int k = 0; k < iterations; k++)
        {
            pi += 1 / Math.Pow(16, k) * 
                  (4 / (8 * k + 1) - 
                   2 / (8 * k + 4) - 
                   1 / (8 * k + 5) - 
                   1 / (8 * k + 6));
        }
        return pi;
    }

    static string XorString(string binaryString)
    {
        char[] result = new char[binaryString.Length];
        for (int i = 0; i < binaryString.Length; i++)
        {
            if (binaryString[i] == '0')
                result[i] = '1';
            else
                result[i] = '0';
        }
        string xorString = new string(result);
        return XorString(xorString);
    }
}
```

Please note that the above code will only work for strings of length up to 2^32-1 (Integer.MAX_VALUE), because the char data type in C# is 16 bits long.

Also note that calculating pi to a high degree of precision is a computationally intensive task and may take a while to complete.

To improve the accuracy of the calculation, you can increase the number of iterations in the `CalculatePi` method. However, keep in mind that this will also increase the time it takes to calculate pi.

The `XorString` method is used to XOR the binary string twice. However, it's worth noting that XORing a string twice is equivalent to doing nothing, because XORing a string with itself results in a string that is the same as the original string.

