Below is an example of a function in Rust to generate a prime number stream using a Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm.

```rust
fn sieve_of_eratosthenes(n: u64) -> impl Iterator<Item = u64> {
    let mut sieve = vec![true; (n + 1) as usize];
    sieve[0] = false;
    sieve[1] = false;

    for i in 2..=(n as f64).sqrt() as u64 {
        if sieve[i as usize] {
            let mut j = i * i;
            while let Some(_) = sieve.get_mut(j as usize) {
                sieve[j as usize] = false;
                j += i;
            }
        }
    }

    sieve.into_iter().enumerate().filter_map(|(i, is_prime)| {
        if is_prime {
            Some(i as u64)
        } else {
            None
        }
    })
}

fn main() {
    let mut primes = sieve_of_eratosthenes(100);

    for _ in 0..10 {
        println!("Next prime number: {}", primes.next().unwrap());
    }
}
```

However, this approach might be memory-intensive for large values of `n` because it first creates a boolean array representing the whole range of numbers up to `n`.

If we want a lazy iterator that generates prime numbers on the fly, we can use a simple function like the following:

```rust
use std::cmp::Ordering;

fn is_prime(mut n: u64) -> bool {
    if n <= 1 {
        return false;
    }

    let mut i = 2;
    while i * i <= n {
        match n % i {
            0 => return false,
            _ => i += 1,
        }
    }
    true
}

fn prime_generator() -> impl Iterator<Item = u64> {
    std::iter::unfold(2, |state| {
        let next = *state;
        *state += 1;
        Some((next, state))
    }).filter(|&n| is_prime(n))
}

fn main() {
    let primes = prime_generator().take(10);

    for prime in primes {
        println!("Next prime number: {}", prime);
    }
}
```

This version generates prime numbers on the fly without storing them in memory, which makes it more memory-efficient for large ranges of numbers.

