**Blocking Read Interrupted by Signal with EINTR Handling**
===========================================================

Below is an example code in C that demonstrates how to handle a blocking read interrupted by a signal, with EINTR handling:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <signal.h>

// Flag to track whether the signal has been received
volatile sig_atomic_t signal_received = 0;

// Signal handler function
void signal_handler(int sig) {
    signal_received = 1;
}

int main() {
    // Set up signal handler
    struct sigaction sa;
    sa.sa_handler = signal_handler;
    sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
    sa.sa_flags = 0;
    if (sigaction(SIGINT, &sa, NULL) == -1) {
        perror("sigaction");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Open a file for reading
    int fd = open("example.txt", O_RDONLY);
    if (fd == -1) {
        perror("open");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    char buffer[1024];
    ssize_t bytes_read;

    while (1) {
        // Perform blocking read
        bytes_read = read(fd, buffer, 1024);

        // Check if the read was interrupted by a signal
        if (bytes_read == -1 && errno == EINTR) {
            // If the signal has been received, exit the loop
            if (signal_received) {
                break;
            }
            // Otherwise, retry the read
            continue;
        } else if (bytes_read == -1) {
            // If the read failed for any other reason, print an error message
            perror("read");
            break;
        } else if (bytes_read == 0) {
            // If the end of the file has been reached, exit the loop
            break;
        }

        // Process the read data
        printf("Read %zd bytes: %.*s\n", bytes_read, (int)bytes_read, buffer);
    }

    // Close the file descriptor
    if (close(fd) == -1) {
        perror("close");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

**Explanation**
---------------

1.  We set up a signal handler for SIGINT using `sigaction`.
2.  We open a file for reading using `open`.
3.  We perform a blocking read using `read` in a loop.
4.  If the read is interrupted by a signal (EINTR), we check if the signal has been received.
5.  If the signal has been received, we exit the loop. Otherwise, we retry the read.
6.  If the read fails for any other reason, we print an error message and exit the loop.
7.  If the end of the file has been reached, we exit the loop.
8.  Finally, we close the file descriptor using `close`.

**Example Use Case**
--------------------

1.  Compile the code using `gcc -o example example.c`.
2.  Run the program using `./example`.
3.  While the program is running, press Ctrl+C to send a SIGINT signal.
4.  Observe that the program handles the signal and exits the loop.

