Here is an example of a blocking read operation that can be interrupted by a signal, along with EINTR handling:
```
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <errno.h>

void sigusr1_handler(int sig) {
    printf("Received SIGUSR1 signal!\n");
}

int main() {
    int fd = open("example.txt", O_RDONLY);
    if (fd == -1) {
        perror("open");
        return 1;
    }

    struct sigaction sa;
    sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
    sa.sa_handler = sigusr1_handler;
    sigaction(SIGUSR1, &sa, NULL);

    char buffer[1024];
    int bytes_read;

    while ((bytes_read = read(fd, buffer, 1024)) == -1 && errno == EINTR) {
        printf("Read interrupted by signal, retrying...\n");
    }

    if (bytes_read == -1) {
        perror("read");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Read %d bytes: %.*s\n", bytes_read, bytes_read, buffer);

    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we open a file descriptor `fd` to a file "example.txt" and set up a signal handler for SIGUSR1 using `sigaction`. The signal handler simply prints a message to the console.

We then enter a loop where we attempt to read up to 1024 bytes from the file descriptor using the `read` system call. If the `read` call returns -1 and `errno` is set to `EINTR`, it means that the read operation was interrupted by a signal (in this case, SIGUSR1). We handle this by printing a message and retrying the read operation.

If the `read` call returns -1 and `errno` is not `EINTR`, we print an error message using `perror` and exit.

If the `read` call succeeds, we print the number of bytes read and the contents of the buffer.

To test this example, you can run the program and then send a SIGUSR1 signal to the process using the `kill` command, like this:
```
$ ./example &
[1] 12345
$ kill -USR1 12345
```
This should cause the program to print "Received SIGUSR1 signal!" and then retry the read operation.

