```matlab
function primes = find_primes(n)
    % FIND_PRIMES returns all prime numbers up to n
    primes = [];
    for p = 2:sqrt(n)
        is_prime = true;
        if p > 2 && mod(p, 2) == 0
            continue;  % even numbers > 2 are not prime
        end
        for i = 3:sqrt(p)
            if mod(p, i) == 0
                is_prime = false;
                break;
            end
        end
        if is_prime
            primes = [primes, p];
        end
    end
    % Use the Sieve of Eratosthenes for larger values of n
    if n > 100
        sieve = true(n, 1);
        sieve(1) = false;  % 1 is not prime
        for p = 2:sqrt(n)
            if sieve(p)
                sieve(p*p:p:n) = false;
            end
        end
        primes = find(sieve);
    end
end
```
Here's an explanation of the algorithm:

1. The function `find_primes` takes an integer `n` as input and returns a vector of all prime numbers up to `n`.
2. For smaller values of `n`, we use a simple trial division method to find prime numbers. We iterate from 2 to `sqrt(n)` and check if each number is prime by dividing it by all numbers up to its square root. If a number is prime, we add it to the `primes` vector.
3. For larger values of `n`, we use the Sieve of Eratosthenes, which is a more efficient algorithm for finding prime numbers. We create a boolean array `sieve` of size `n`, where `sieve(i)` represents whether `i` is prime or not. We initialize the array with all `true` values, except for `sieve(1)`, which is set to `false` since 1 is not prime.
4. We iterate from 2 to `sqrt(n)` and mark all multiples of each prime number as non-prime (i.e., `sieve(p*p:p:n) = false`). This is done using the fact that all non-prime numbers can be represented as a product of prime numbers.
5. Finally, we return the indices of the `true` values in the `sieve` array, which correspond to the prime numbers.

