Here is a piece of Java code that uses Java Streams to check if a list has not more than one entry. It returns the single entry if the list contains exactly one element, null if the list is empty, and throws an exception if the list contains more than one element.

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class Main {

    public static <T> T getSingleEntry(List<T> list) {
        if (list == null) {
            return null;
        }

        return list.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.collectingAndThen(
                        Collectors.toList(),
                        entries -> {
                            if (entries.size() > 1) {
                                throw new IllegalStateException("More than one entry found");
                            }
                            return entries.isEmpty() ? null : entries.get(0);
                        }
                ));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example usage:
        List<String> list1 = List.of("a", "b");
        try {
            System.out.println(getSingleEntry(list1)); // More than one entry found
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage()); // More than one entry found
        }

        List<String> list2 = List.of("a");
        System.out.println(getSingleEntry(list2)); // a

        List<String> list3 = List.of();
        System.out.println(getSingleEntry(list3)); // null
    }
}
```

However, it's often more idiomatic to use `Optional` when dealing with a result that might be absent. Here's how you could modify the code to return an `Optional`:

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class Main {

    public static <T> Optional<T> getSingleEntry(List<T> list) {
        if (list == null) {
            return Optional.empty();
        }

        return list.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.collectingAndThen(
                        Collectors.toList(),
                        entries -> {
                            if (entries.size() > 1) {
                                throw new IllegalStateException("More than one entry found");
                            }
                            return entries.isEmpty() ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(entries.get(0));
                        }
                ));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example usage:
        List<String> list1 = List.of("a", "b");
        try {
            System.out.println(getSingleEntry(list1)); // More than one entry found
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage()); // More than one entry found
        }

        List<String> list2 = List.of("a");
        System.out.println(getSingleEntry(list2).orElse("default")); // a

        List<String> list3 = List.of();
        System.out.println(getSingleEntry(list3).orElse("default")); // default
    }
