To extract text from a document in a web context using JavaScript, you can select all the text nodes within the document and then concatenate them into a single string. This function will iterate over all the nodes in the document, check if they are text nodes, and then collect their text content.

Here's a simple JavaScript function that does this:

```javascript
function extractTextFromDocument() {
  // Select all the text nodes in the document
  const textNodes = document.body.querySelectorAll('*::text');
  let documentText = '';

  // Iterate over the text nodes and concatenate their text content
  textNodes.forEach(node => {
    documentText += node.textContent.trim() + ' ';
  });

  // Return the concatenated text
  return documentText;
}
```

This function uses `querySelectorAll('*::text')` to select all text nodes in the document. It then iterates over these nodes using `forEach`, trims any leading or trailing whitespace from their text content, and appends the text to the `documentText` variable with a space in between each text node's content.

