To create a Rust program that reads URLs from a file, separates YouTube URLs from others, and downloads YouTube videos using `yt-dlp`, you can follow the steps below. Note that this code assumes you have `yt-dlp` installed and available in your system's PATH.

First, you'll need to add the necessary dependencies to your `Cargo.toml` file:

```toml
[package]
name = "url_downloader"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2018"

[dependencies]
reqwest = { version = "0.11", features = ["json"] }
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
serde = "1"
serde_derive = "1"
```

However, the `reqwest` and `tokio` libraries are not necessary for this specific task. We're going to use `std::fs` and `std::process` to read files and execute commands. Here's how to implement the program:

1. Create a new Rust project with `cargo new url_downloader`.
2. Replace your `src/main.rs` with the following code:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};
use std::process::Command;

fn is_youtube_url(url: &str) -> bool {
    url.starts_with("https://www.youtube.com/") ||
    url.starts_with("https://youtube.com/") ||
    url.starts_with("https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/") ||
    url.starts_with("https://m.youtube.com/")
}

fn download_youtube_video(url: &str) {
    let status = Command::new("yt-dlp")
                         .arg(url)
                         .status()
                         .expect("Failed to execute yt-dlp");
    if !status.success() {
        println!("Failed to download video: {}", url);
    } else {
        println!("Video downloaded: {}", url);
    }
}

fn main() {
    let file = File::open("urls.txt").expect("Failed to open file");
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    for line in reader.lines() {
        if let Ok(url) = line {
            if is_youtube_url(&url) {
                download_youtube_video(&url);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This program does the following:
- It reads from a file named `urls.txt` in the current directory.
- It checks each line to see if it's a YouTube URL.
- If it is a YouTube URL, it downloads the video using `yt-dlp`.

Make sure you have a `urls.txt` file in the same directory as your Rust program, and fill it with URLs, one per line.

Before running the program, ensure you have `yt-dlp` installed on your system. You can check this by running `yt-dlp --version` in your terminal.

Finally, run the program with `cargo run`.

