In Jetpack Compose, you can achieve layout behavior similar to container queries by using `Modifier.layout` and creating a custom layout modifier. Container queries allow you to style elements based on the size of their container, and while Jetpack Compose doesn't directly support this concept, you can mimic it by measuring the size of the parent and applying different layouts to children based on that size.

To create a composable that changes the content and position of its child depending on the available space, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a custom layout modifier that measures the parent's size.
2. Within the modifier, based on the available size, decide which layout logic to apply to the children.
3. Use this modifier when composing your UI.

Here's a high-level example:

```kotlin
import androidx.compose.foundation.layout.Box
import androidx.compose.foundation.layout.fillMaxSize
import androidx.compose.material.Text
import androidx.compose.runtime.Composable
import androidx.compose.ui.Modifier
import androidx.compose.ui.layout.Layout
import androidx.compose.ui.layout.Measurable
import androidx.compose.ui.layout.MeasureResult
import androidx.compose.ui.layout.MeasureScope
import androidx.compose.ui.unit.Constraints
import androidx.compose.ui.unit.Dp
import androidx.compose.ui.unit.dp

@Composable
fun AdaptiveContent(modifier: Modifier = Modifier) {
    // Use the custom modifier
    Box(
        modifier = modifier
            .fillMaxSize()
            .adaptiveLayout()
    ) {
        Text("Hello, world!")
    }
}

// Custom Modifier
fun Modifier.adaptiveLayout(): Modifier = this.then(Layout { measurable, constraints ->
    val placeables = measurable.measure(constraints)

    val width = constraints.maxWidth
    val height = constraints.maxHeight

    // Here you can decide the layout logic based on width and height
    val position: Dp = if (width > 300.dp) {
        // Wide screen, position at the center
        100.dp
    } else {
        // Small screen, position at the top
        0.dp
    }

    layout(constraints.maxWidth, constraints.maxHeight) {
        placeables.placeRelative(x = position.toPx(), y = position.toPx())
    }
})
```

In this example, the `AdaptiveContent` composable uses a `Box` with a custom modifier `adaptiveLayout`. This modifier measures the size and, based on the width, changes the position of the child `Text` composable.

Please note that in this simple example, the position is changed based on the width being greater than 300dp. You can extend and customize this logic to fit your specific needs, such as changing the layout of multiple children or applying different styles based on the size.

Remember that the `Layout` modifier can be complex and might require more sophisticated logic depending on the requirements of your UI.

