In PHP 8.2, you can manage the maximum number of running coroutines by using the `Channel` class from the `channels` package. Channels can be used to limit the concurrency of coroutine execution. Here's an example of how you can create a limited concurrency job runner:

1. First, make sure you have the `channels` package installed. If you don't have it, you can install it via Composer:
```bash
composer require php:channels
```
1. Create a file named `LimitedCoroutineRunner.php` and add the following code:
```php
<?php

namespace App;

use function Amp\{call, \chan, \close};
use function Amp\Coroutine;
use function Amp\Promise;

class LimitedCoroutineRunner
{
    private $jobQueue;
    private $workerPool;
    private $maxConcurrentJobs = 5;

    public function __construct()
    {
        $this->jobQueue = chan($this->maxConcurrentJobs);
        $this->workerPool = [];
    }

    public function enqueueJob(callable $job, mixed $data = null): Promise
    {
        return $this->jobQueue->enqueue([fn () use ($job, $data) => $job($data)]);
    }

    public function run(): void
    {
        $this->startWorkerPool();
    }

    private function startWorkerPool(): void
    {
        for ($i = 0; $i < $this->maxConcurrentJobs; $i++) {
            $this->workerPool[] = call(function () {
                while (($job = yield $this->jobQueue->receive())) {
                    try {
                        yield $job();
                    } catch (\Exception $e) {
                        // You can log the exception or handle it differently based on your needs
                        \error_log("Job failed: " . $e->getMessage());
                    } finally {
                        $this->jobQueue->send(null);
                    }
                }
            });
        }
    }

    public function stop(): void
    {
        foreach ($this->workerPool as $worker) {
            $worker->resolve();
        }

        close($this->jobQueue);
    }
}
```
1. Now you can use the `LimitedCoroutineRunner` class to manage your coroutines:
```php
