In this example, I'll provide a simple implementation of a Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) using plain TypeScript. This implementation will be a basic RNN cell without any specific activation function, loss function, or optimization. You can extend it to include these features as per your requirement.

```typescript
// Define a simple RNN cell class
class SimpleRNNCell {
  private weights: number[][];
  private bias: number[];

  constructor(inputSize: number, hiddenSize: number) {
    // Initialize random weights and bias
    this.weights = Array(hiddenSize)
      .fill(null)
      .map(() => Array(inputSize + hiddenSize).fill(Math.random() - 0.5));
    this.bias = Array(hiddenSize).fill(Math.random() - 0.5);
  }

  // Forward pass through the RNN cell
  public forward(input: number[]): number[] {
    const previousHiddenState = input.slice(-1);
    const combinedInput = [
      ...input.slice(0, -1),
      ...previousHiddenState,
    ];

    const hiddenState = combinedInput.map((x, i) => this.weights[i][x] + this.bias[i]);
    return hiddenState;
  }
}

// Use the RNN cell
const inputSequence = [0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9];
const hiddenSize = 5;
const rnnCell = new SimpleRNNCell(inputSequence.length - 1, hiddenSize);

// Process the input sequence
let hiddenState = hiddenSize.map(() => 0);
inputSequence.forEach((input, index) => {
  hiddenState = rnnCell.forward([...hiddenState, input]);
  console.log(`Hidden state at step ${index + 1}:`, hiddenState);
});
```

This example demonstrates a simple RNN cell with no activation function. The `SimpleRNNCell` class has a constructor that initializes random weights and bias for the cell. The `forward` method takes an input sequence and calculates the next hidden state by combining the current input and the previous hidden state.

