Here's a recursive JavaScript function that retries a request up to 3 times before resolving the promise without using `await`. This example uses the `fetch` API for the request, but you can replace it with any function that returns a promise.
```javascript
function retryRequest(url, retryCount = 3) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    fetch(url)
      .then(response => {
        if (response.ok) {
          resolve(response);
        } else {
          if (retryCount > 0) {
            setTimeout(() => retryRequest(url, retryCount - 1), 1000);
          } else {
            reject(new Error('Max retry limit reached'));
          }
        }
      })
      .catch(error => {
        if (retryCount > 0) {
          setTimeout(() => retryRequest(url, retryCount - 1), 1000);
        } else {
          reject(error);
        }
      });
  });
}

// Usage:
const url = 'https://api.example.com/data';
retryRequest(url)
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log('Data:', data))
  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));
```
In this function, `retryRequest` takes the `url` of the request and an optional `retryCount` parameter, which defaults to 3. The function returns a promise that resolves with the successful response or rejects with an error after 3 failed attempts. The function retries the request by calling itself with a reduced `retryCount` after a 1-second delay using `setTimeout`.

