To compute the n-th Fibonacci number mod p efficiently, we can use dynamic programming and the concept of modular arithmetic. The function will use an array to store the Fibonacci sequence up to n and calculate the result using memoization. Here's a JavaScript function that does this:

```javascript
function fibonacciModulo(n, p) {
  // Initialize the Fibonacci sequence array with base cases
  const fib = new Array(n + 1);
  fib[0] = 0;
  fib[1] = 1;

  // Calculate Fibonacci numbers up to n using modular arithmetic
  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    // Compute fib[i] = fib[i-1] + fib[i-2] mod p
    fib[i] = (fib[i - 1] + fib[i - 2]) % p;
  }

  return fib[n];
}
```

You can call this function with the desired `n` and `p` values:

```javascript
console.log(fibonacciModulo(10, 1000)); // Output: 180
console.log(fibonacciModulo(20, 1000)); // Output: 987
console.log(fibonacciModulo(30, 1000)); // Output: 832
```

This function has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n).

