In PHP, coroutines are typically managed using the Swoole extension. Swoole provides a powerful event-driven architecture for writing asynchronous, concurrent, and high-performance applications. Here's how you can limit the maximum number of running coroutines to 5 using Swoole in PHP 8.2:

1. First, make sure you have installed the Swoole extension for PHP. You can install it using Composer or by compiling it from source.

2. Create a function that starts a coroutine and keeps track of the running coroutines:

```php
function startCoroutine($job, &$runningCoroutines = [])
{
    if (count($runningCoroutines) >= 5) {
        echo "Max coroutines limit reached, cannot start more coroutines.\n";
        return;
    }

    $runningCoroutines[] = \Swoole\Coroutine::create(function () use ($job) {
        // Your coroutine logic here
        $job();
        // When the coroutine finishes, remove it from the array
        array_pop($runningCoroutines);
    });
}
```

3. Write your coroutine job function. This is the function that will be executed in the coroutine:

```php
function job()
{
    // Your job logic here
    sleep(1);
    echo "Coroutine finished its job.\n";
}
```

4. Now, you can add jobs to the coroutine queue while maintaining the max limit:

```php
$start = microtime(true);

$jobs = [
    function () { job(); },
    function () { job(); },
    function () { job(); },
    function () { job(); },
    function () { job(); },
    function () { job(); },
    function () { job(); },
];

foreach ($jobs as $job) {
    startCoroutine($job, $runningCoroutines);
}

// Wait for all coroutines to finish
\Swoole\Coroutine\WaitGroup::wait($runningCoroutines);

$end = microtime(true);
echo "All coroutines finished in " . ($end - $start) . " seconds.\n";
```

In this example, if you try to start more than 5 coroutines, the `startCoroutine` function will check the `$runningCoroutines` array's length and prevent starting a new coroutine until one of the existing coroutines completes. The `WaitGroup::wait()` function is used to block the main thread until all coroutines have finished.

Please note that Swoole is not part of the standard PHP distribution, so you'll need to install it separately.

