Calculating the value of pi using JavaScript can be done through various algorithms, one of which is the Monte Carlo method. This method estimates the value of pi by generating random points in a square and determining how many of them fall within a quarter-circle inscribed within the square.

Here's one way to implement this method in JavaScript:

```javascript
// Define the number of points to generate
const numPoints = 1000000;

// Define the size of the square
const squareSize = 1;

// Define the radius of the quarter-circle
const circleRadius = Math.sqrt(squareSize / 4);

// Initialize the counters
let totalPoints = 0;
let insideCirclePoints = 0;

// Generate random points within the square
for (let i = 0; i < numPoints; i++) {
 const x = Math.random() * squareSize;
 const y = Math.random() * squareSize;

 // Determine if the point falls within the quarter-circle
 if (Math.sqrt(x * x + y * y) <= circleRadius) {
    insideCirclePoints++;
 }

 // Increment the total points counter
 totalPoints++;
}

// Calculate and print the estimated value of pi
const estimatedPi = ((4 * insideCirclePoints) / totalPoints) * (squareSize * squareSize);
console.log(`Estimated value of pi: ${estimatedPi}`);
```

This code generates a specified number of random points within a square, and then calculates how many of those points fall within a quarter-circle inscribed within the square. The value of pi is then estimated using the ratio of the number of points within the circle to the total number of points, multiplied by the size of the square squared.

Keep in mind that the accuracy of this method depends on the number of points generated, so increasing the value of `numPoints` will result in a more accurate estimate of pi.

