To create a Sierpinski Triangle in XAML, you can use a `Canvas` element and add `Polygon` elements for each level of the triangle. Here's a basic example that demonstrates this approach:

1. Create a `Window` or a `UserControl` in your WPF project.
2. Add the following XAML code to the `Window` or `UserControl`:
```xaml
<Window
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    Title="Sierpinski Triangle" Height="400" Width="400">
    <Canvas x:Name="canvas">
        <Polygon 
            Points="0,0 0.5,{bind Height} 1,0"
            Fill="Black"
            Stroke="Black"
            StrokeThickness="1"
            Margin="50,50,0,0"
            RenderTransformOrigin="0.5,0.5">
            <Polygon.RenderTransform>
                <ScaleTransform ScaleX="1" ScaleY="{Binding ActualHeight, ElementName=canvas}" />
            </Polygon.RenderTransform>
        </Polygon>
    </Canvas>
</Window>
```
1. Add a `Code-behind` file to your project, and add the following C# code to it:
```csharp
using System.Windows;

namespace SierpinskiTriangle
{
    public partial class MainWindow : Window
    {
        public MainWindow()
        {
            InitializeComponent();
            Loaded += MainWindow_Loaded;
        }

        private void MainWindow_Loaded(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
        {
            RecursiveSierpinskiTriangle(canvas, 5);
        }

        private void RecursiveSierpinskiTriangle(Canvas canvas, int level)
        {
            if (level == 0) return;

            double sideLength = canvas.ActualHeight / 2.0;
            double third = sideLength / 3.0;

            Polygon topLeftTriangle = CreateTriangle(0, 0, 0.5 * third, 0.5 * third, 0);
            Polygon topRightTriangle = CreateTriangle(0.5 * third, 0.5 * third, 0.5 * sideLength, 0.5 * sideLength, 0);
            Polygon bottomLeftTriangle = CreateTriangle(0, sideLength, 0.5 * third, sideLength + 0.5 * third, 0);
            Polygon bottomRightTriangle = CreateTriangle(0.5 * third, sideLength + 0.5 * third, 0.5 * sideLength, 0.5 * sideLength + sideLength, 0);

            canvas.Children.Add(topLeftTriangle);
            canvas.Children.Add(topRightTriangle);
            canvas.Children.Add(bottomLeftTriangle);
            canvas.Children.Add(bottomRightTriangle);

            RecursiveSierpinskiTriangle(canvas, level - 1);
        }

        private Polygon CreateTriangle(double x1, double y1, double x2, double y2, double x3, double y3 = 0)
        {
            Polygon triangle = new Polygon();
            triangle.Points = new PointCollection { new Point(x1, y1), new Point(x2, y2), new Point(x3, y3) };
            triangle.Fill = Brushes.Black;
            triangle.Stroke = Brushes.Black;
            triangle.StrokeThickness = 1;
            return triangle;
        }
    }
}
```
This example creates a Sierpinski Triangle using a `Canvas` and recursive `Polygon` elements. The `RecursiveSierpinskiTriangle` method creates triangles for the specified level and recursively calls itself for the next levels. The `MainWindow_Loaded` event is used to trigger the recursion.

You can customize the size and level of the Sierpinski Triangle by tweaking the values in the XAML code or the `RecursiveSierpinskiTriangle` method.

