Currently, Jetpack Compose does not have built-in support for container queries like we have in web development. However, you can achieve a similar effect by using the ConstraintLayout in Jetpack Compose.

Here's an example of how you can create a composable that changes the content and position of its child based on the available space using ConstraintLayout:

```kotlin
import androidx.compose.foundation.layout.ConstraintLayout
import androidx.compose.foundation.layout.Dimension
import androidx.compose.runtime.Composable
import androidx.compose.ui.Modifier
import androidx.compose.ui.graphics.Color
import androidx.compose.ui.unit.dp

@Composable
fun AdaptiveLayout() {
    ConstraintLayout(
        modifier = Modifier.fillMaxSize()
    ) {
        val (topItem, bottomItem) = createRefs()

        Box(
            modifier = Modifier
                .constrainAs(topItem) {
                    top.linkTo(parent.top)
                    start.linkTo(parent.start)
                    end.linkTo(parent.end)
                    width = Dimension.fillToConstraints
                    height = Dimension.wrapContent
                }
        ) {
            // Content for top item
        }

        Box(
            modifier = Modifier
                .constrainAs(bottomItem) {
                    top.linkTo(topItem.bottom)
                    start.linkTo(parent.start)
                    end.linkTo(parent.end)
                    width = Dimension.fillToConstraints
                    height = Dimension.wrapContent
                }
        ) {
            // Content for bottom item
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the AdaptiveLayout composable uses ConstraintLayout to position two child items (top item and bottom item). You can adjust the constraints and sizes of the child items based on available space to achieve the desired layout changes.

Remember that Jetpack Compose is evolving rapidly, and future updates may introduce new features or libraries that provide more advanced layout capabilities.

